From 423bbbcdb7d20242051a34ff7b6b272e7dac6bac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilan Joselevich <personal@ilanjoselevich.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 00:48:26 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 17/17] client, scheduler: count how much of each requested
 graph is cached

The scheduler's cache_hits and cache_misses count Wants it looked up,
but a client Wants only what it found missing: it walks the graph top
down and skips every derivation whose needed outputs the server already
holds or substitutes. So the scheduler mostly sees misses, and its hit
ratio sits low however warm the cache is. How much of what clients
needed was cached is a figure only the client sees.

The walk now counts the derivations whose outputs it needed, and of
those the ones it found held. Nothing below a held derivation is
needed, so a cached subtree counts once, as its top. The client sends
both in a new ClientMsg, Graph, once per graph, ahead of its first
Wants. Older schedulers skip a ClientMsg they do not know.

When nothing is left to build, the client opens a Schedule stream only
for the counts and half-closes it, which the scheduler ends. The counts
are best-effort statistics, so a fully cached build waits for that 2 s
at most, GrpcStore::graphReportDeadline. Past it, or on any error, the
counts are lost, not retried, the build's own result stands, and a
debug line says how many reports the client has dropped so far.

The scheduler sums them into two new counters, graph_drvs and
graph_pruned_drvs, which reach Stats totals and the counter history.
One Graph adds at most 2^24 derivations, and no more pruned than
considered.

cache_hits and cache_misses stay, documented as what they are: Wants
found cached, or not, when they reached the scheduler, where a hit is
mostly a race won between clients. A Want for a derivation already
tracked joins it, queued or building, and now counts as neither; it
used to count as a miss.

top-down-test covers the counts on a graph with a cached subtree.
graph-report-test sends a report to a scheduler that never answers,
which costs the deadline at most, and to one that answers or is down,
which ends it at once. events-test covers the counters summed over two
clients, and its randomized run models the misses of Wants that join.
